in march 2020 one man began  memorializing people who died from covid  on the twitter account faces of covet is  what he called it his name is alex  goldstein and he's paid tribute to  thousands of people  every day he highlights five or six  stories in a conversation  with our lead national correspondent  david bagno he explained why these  tributes are so very important to family  members who have lost loved ones and are  so meaningful to him as well  david good morning looking forward to  the story good morning my friend you  know one of the remarkable things about  alex goldstein is that he knows a little  bit about thousands of people's lives  he's been memorializing covet victims  online nearly every day for more than  two years in fact he said david the only  time i took a break was when my dad died  of prostate cancer  other than that he's done it every  single day  alex goldstein has waited nearly a year  and a half oh my god to meet karen  nassim benny in the flesh  how are you i'm okay how are you  i'm good better now for seeing you in  the flesh you know i spent too long in  the making i've never met anybody  from faces of covet it's always just  been from behind the screen and so  you're the first person  i've actually met whose loved one was on  the platform you were just kidding micah  i know how lucky i feel honored  karen's late husband stephen richard a  kind funny gentle soul whose heart and  hands were always open to helping others  was memorialized on the faces of coveted  twitter account which was created by  goldstein  as a way to personalize the pandemic's  toll no one else has put  a face to these victims the way you have  otherwise they're just a number you've  brought humanity  to this god-awful pandemic  you tell me a little more about stephen  he was one in a million we had a mad  love affair you know for the 30 years  that we were together he was salt of the  earth he was a kayaker he was a  photographer and he was the type that  could be really invisible so he always  got the most poignant shots how did you  meet  we met through a mutual friend and i  looked at him and i thought oh my god  he's the boy next door this is the kind  of normal guy i should like  goldstein has featured more than seven  thousand stories on faces of covet i try  to  wake up each morning look at the  submissions that have come in from  family members do a bit of my own  research i've done it almost every  single day  since march 28 2020 it has  become  more of a  required ritual in my life than i think  i could have ever anticipated in many  posts it is the picture which gives him  pause  all that faces of kovid tries to do is  get folks to stop just for a minute and  bear witness and look at their faces and  say their names um  and create just a little space for them  i love this one this quote that the  family gave he was never one for  religiosity but he wanted you to know  that if you find him in heaven he will  gladly accept a good beer and a juicy  steak brazilian plays american food is  terrible i mean this is really  vulnerable stuff  you are opening up on the  most famously toxic platform in social  media you are putting your loved one out  there with a couple lines about who they  were and then you're holding your breath  to see what the world does with it  and the things that people share it just  always blows me away this is like a  digital cemetery  you just put some of the quotes that i  wish headstones had on them right  it's a it's a good way to put it  roughly 5 000 people saw the face of  linda benson of oklahoma city this was  one where the the photo hit me  just this kind of relaxed smile you talk  about what would you want on your  tombstone as a parent  my brother and i were raised and taught  by a wise lady and we are forever  grateful for her love she was a sweet  sensitive and encouraging person i mean  you know  what else could you want as your legacy  this one uh was particularly powerful  because so this is dr chandra  57 of ohio  he was a cardiologist  and  i had found his story  through a news article  and his son reached out and saw it on  faces of covid and said can i add some  words what were the words his son wanted  to add he said that dr chandra was a  fierce advocate for his patients and for  the health of his community he spent his  life educating others on preventative  medicine  caretaking  to him was not a profession but a  calling he approached everyone as equals  with kindness and love he is remembered  fondly by his community one that  stretches three continents  the 38 year old goldstein who spent his  late 20s as a press secretary for a  former massachusetts governor  sees the faces of covid twitter account  as a first draft of history this history  is unfolding right now as we speak you  feel protective about it oh yeah  i feel a responsibility to make sure  that these people are treated with  dignity and respect i heard someone say  over the course of the pandemic in  speaking about someone who died of covet  may their memory be a blessing  you are preserving memories  i'm trying  um  it is too easy  in this pandemic if it hasn't happened  to you to look the other way  and we need to be uncomfortable and  vulnerable enough to sit with it and to  look at it and to say the names and look  at the pictures i know how hard it is i  feel it every morning  but the alternative  it's not acceptable  what has been the impact of this twitter  account you know what i hope the the  impact of faces of covet has been is  to restore  some semblance of empathy into how we  see each other anyone can come here  and say goodbye and  watch a bunch of strangers  participate in that with them and i  think there's meaning in that i hope  there's meaning in that  all those faces and so many more  you know one story we didn't highlight  was this there was a woman who reached  out to alex saying that she was thinking  about her dad hysterically as she termed  i